## The problem

`GRADE_CEILINGS = { A: 5, B: 10, C: 20, D: 30 }` in `src/lib/models/catalog.ts` grades models by word error rate using the **US school letter scale**, with `+`/`−` suffixes rendered in `GradeChip.tsx`.

Handy ships 27 locales (`ru`, `cs`, `pl`, `uk`, `de`, `fr`, `ja`, …), but the letters are hardcoded:

```ts
const LETTER_CLASSES: Record<GradeLetter, string> = { A: ..., B: ..., C: ..., D: ..., F: ... };
```

Two problems for non-US users:

1. **The letter carries no information on its own.** "B" is only meaningful if you already know the US convention *and* know the cut-offs. A user has to open the legend to learn that B means 5–10% error.
2. **It is not a neutral scale.** In Czechia, Poland, Ukraine, Russia, Germany and much of Europe, students are graded 1–5 or 1–6, often with 1 as the best mark — the opposite direction. Seeing "F" for the worst model and "A" for the best is fine, but seeing "B−" next to a percentage the user cannot see is a puzzle, not information.

## What I'd suggest

Make the **error percentage** the primary label, and keep the letter as a secondary signal:

```
5.2%   ← primary, unambiguous in every language, no legend needed
B      ← small/secondary, or tooltip only
```

Concretely, any of these would fix it:

- **Preferred:** show `error %` in the chip (e.g. `5.2%`), with the letter in the tooltip. Percentage is the actual measurement; a grade is a lossy projection of it.
- Or add a setting: `Grade style: Letters (US) / Percent / Qualitative` — where qualitative is a translated word (`Excellent / Good / Fair / Poor`), which localises naturally.
- Or at minimum, localise the grade scale per locale via i18n so the legend is not the only place the cut-offs appear, and drop `+`/`−` (the `−` had to be special-cased: `label.replace("-", "−")`).

Percentages also fix a second issue: `A` covers everything under 5%, so a 0.4% model and a 4.9% model look identical, even though the difference matters a lot at that end of the scale.

Happy to send a PR for the percent-as-primary variant if you want it — it touches `GradeChip.tsx`, `rubric.ts` and the `modelTable` columns.
Trying to turn thinking off for post-processing, but the retry turns it back on.

## Bug Description

On `main` (`7bfb2e2`), post-processing asks the endpoint to skip reasoning with `reasoning_effort: "none"` (`src-tauri/src/llm_client.rs:79-84`). When the endpoint answers 400/422 because it does not accept that value, the retry added in #1809 resends the same request with no reasoning field at all, and remembers the rejection so no reasoning field is ever sent to that endpoint again.

The issue is what "no reasoning field" means. DeepSeek's API reference documents `thinking` as an object with possible values `enabled` / `disabled` and a default of `enabled`, and `reasoning_effort` as `none` / `low` / `high` / `max` where `none` disables thinking:

- https://api-docs.deepseek.com/api/create-chat-completion
- https://api-docs.deepseek.com/guides/thinking_mode

So the successful retry does not mean reasoning is off, it means the endpoint falls back to its default, which is thinking on. Every dictation after the first one then runs in thinking mode. The rejection is cached in `reasoning_rejections()` for the life of the process (`llm_client.rs:87-110`) and is only logged at `info!`, so nothing in the app shows that post-processing is slower than intended.

Handy already knows a second documented spelling, `thinking: {type: "disabled"}`, but it only sends that when the base URL contains `api.deepseek.com` (`llm_client.rs:62-68`). Any OpenAI compatible gateway or proxy in front of DeepSeek takes the generic path and never gets it.

Measured by replaying Handy's request body (system prompt plus transcript as the user message, `stream: false`), 90 word dictation, median of 2 to 3 runs:

| endpoint | reasoning field sent | result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| gateway | none, after the cached rejection | 200, thinking on, 3.55 s, 133 to 209 reasoning tokens |
| gateway | `reasoning_effort: "none"`, what Handy sends | 400 `Invalid option: expected one of "off"\|"low"\|"medium"\|"high"\|"xhigh"\|"max"` |
| gateway | `reasoning_effort: "off"`, for reference | 200, thinking off, 3.12 s, 0 reasoning tokens |
| gateway | `thinking: {type: "disabled"}` | 200, field ignored, thinking on |
| `api.deepseek.com` | none | 200, thinking on, 1.60 s |
