  - forced onto the Intel iGPU: **6.70 s / 6.85 s** (encode ≈ 5.5 s)
  - forced onto the RTX 5090: **0.17 s / 0.10 s**
- Restarting Handy fixes it (~0.2 s per clip) until the order changes again before the next first model load.

## Root cause (from reading the code)

In `ggml/src/ggml-vulkan/ggml-vulkan.cpp` as vendored in transcribe.cpp v0.2.4:

1. `ggml_backend_vk_reg()` caches `description`, `is_integrated_gpu` and `pci_bus_id` per index at registration time ([L15857-L15862](https://github.com/handy-computer/transcribe.cpp/blob/4807edaf210d0d7e8a6f7fb2a44b65966a2797f0/ggml/src/ggml-vulkan/ggml-vulkan.cpp#L15857-L15862)). Auto selects the discrete GPU from these cached properties, here `Vulkan1` = "RTX 5090".
2. `ggml_vk_get_device(idx)` creates the device later and does ([L3806-L3825](https://github.com/handy-computer/transcribe.cpp/blob/4807edaf210d0d7e8a6f7fb2a44b65966a2797f0/ggml/src/ggml-vulkan/ggml-vulkan.cpp#L3806-L3825)):
   ```cpp
   size_t dev_num = vk_instance.device_indices[idx];
   std::vector<vk::PhysicalDevice> physical_devices = vk_instance.instance.enumeratePhysicalDevices();
   device->physical_device = physical_devices[dev_num];
   ```
   If the loader's order changed since registration, `dev_num` now refers to the other GPU, while the cached description still says "RTX 5090".
3. The created `vk_device` is cached in `vk_instance.devices[idx]` and never released (cf. #1775). That's why unloading and reloading the model keeps using the wrong GPU until the app restarts.

On Windows the loader orders physical devices according to the per-app GPU preference. With handy.exe set to "Power saving" it returns Intel first, and with "High performance" it returns NVIDIA first. The preference is read once per process, and changing it while the process runs does not reorder anything. With no preference set, the order can change during the process lifetime, as it did here. I couldn't trigger that reorder on demand in a test process, so the display-change trigger is inferred from the timeline.

The same `ggml_vk_get_device()` pattern is still in upstream llama.cpp master ([ggml-org/llama.cpp@0ec4027, L4016-L4037](https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/blob/0ec4027a2a0183de87c007069adddc06e665f7de/ggml/src/ggml-vulkan/ggml-vulkan.cpp#L4016-L4037)). It might also explain the boot-dependent "Auto is much slower than CPU" reports in #1884.

## Workaround

Set handy.exe to **High performance** in Windows Settings → System → Display → Graphics, then restart Handy. The registry equivalent is `HKCU\Software\Microsoft\DirectX\UserGpuPreferences`, value `%LOCALAPPDATA%\Handy\handy.exe` = `GpuPreference=2;`. This pins the dGPU first for the whole process. After the restart the log shows `bound backend 'Vulkan0'` (the 5090) and transcription takes 0.18 s for a 1.41 s clip. I haven't re-tested a monitor switch with this setting yet.

From the code, choosing the GPU manually in Handy's settings wouldn't help: the stored device key resolves to the same cached index, and the device is then created by re-enumeration the same way.

## Suggested fix

- **ggml-vulkan:** keep the `vk::PhysicalDevice` handles from the initial enumeration, or re-resolve by a stable identity such as `deviceUUID`, LUID or PCI bus id, instead of re-enumerating and indexing by number. `ggml_backend_vk_get_device_memory()` and `ggml_backend_vk_get_device_type()` also re-enumerate by index, so cached and live properties can disagree.
- **Handy / transcribe.cpp:** log the name of the physical device that was actually created, rather than the cached registration description, so a mismatch shows up in the logs.
