## Evidence

### Handy log (v0.9.7, both "Default" and explicitly selected mic)
```
[recorder][INFO] Using device: Ok("Микрофон (Realtek(R) Audio)")
Sample rate: 48000
Channels: 2
Format: F32
... first audio samples arrived ... after stream start (20.0ms drained)
...
VAD at stop: withheld tail ... 0 voiced
Recording stopped and samples retrieved in ..., sample count: 0
Recording produced no audio samples; skipping persistence
```
So the capture stream runs, callback fires, ring buffer fills — but every frame is silent → VAD 0 voiced → nothing persisted, level meter dead.

### Independent WASAPI shared-mode capture test (C# / NAudio 2.2.1), same device
| client format (shared mode) | result |
|---|---|
| mix format (48000/32-bit/extensible) | **signal** |
| 32-bit PCM `WAVEFORMATEX` 48000/2 | **signal** |
| IEEE float 48000/2 (also with/without `AUTOCONVERTPCM`) | **all zeros, no error** |
| IEEE float 44100/2, IEEE float 48000/1 mono | **all zeros** |
| 16-bit PCM 48000/2 | **all zeros** |
| exclusive mode (any format) | init error `E_INVALIDARG` |

Only the exact native mix format and 32-bit PCM delivered real audio; every differing client format initialized successfully but produced pure zeros.

## Root cause (hypothesis)

`AudioRecorder::get_preferred_config` deliberately prioritises sample formats:
```rust
// Prioritize F32 > I16 > I32 > others
let score = |fmt: cpal::SampleFormat| match fmt {
    cpal::SampleFormat::F32 => 4,
    cpal::SampleFormat::I16 => 3,
    cpal::SampleFormat::I32 => 2,
    _ => 1,
};
```
On WASAPI shared mode the audio engine buffers should contain the mix stream in the client's requested format, but on this driver an IEEE-float client gets an all-zero buffer while a PCM/mix-format client gets real audio. cpal opens F32 without error, logs `Format: F32`, and Handy then processes silence as if it were speech input.

## Suggested fix (upstream)

In `get_preferred_config` (`src-tauri/src/audio_toolkit/audio/recorder.rs`), prefer the device's native `default_input_config()` (the mix format) on Windows/WASAPI instead of hunting for F32 — i.e. stop overriding the mix format selection with the F32 preference. F32 preference (from #393) was added for quality on platforms where it maps to the device's native format; on Windows it can request a client format that Realtek's APO graph does not actually deliver.

At minimum: if the device's `default_input_config()` sample format differs from the "best" scored format, use the default config on Windows.

