## Notes

Since the overlay worked in 0.8.3 and broke in 0.9.0, the regression was likely introduced in that specific release. Bisecting between those two tags may help isolate the commit. ---

## Description

The CLI table in `AGENTS.md` documents:

> `--no-tray` — Launch without system tray (closing window quits the app)

In the current code, no close path quits: the `CloseRequested` handler
calls `prevent_close()` + `window.hide()` unconditionally, and there is no
`no_tray` check anywhere in the close/hide decision (the flag is only read
at startup for tray setup). Nothing calls `app.exit()` on window close —
the only `app.exit(0)` is the tray Quit menu, which does not exist under
`--no-tray`.

## Impact

With `--no-tray`:

- macOS: hidden window is recoverable via the dock icon.
- Windows/GNOME/KDE: recoverable via the taskbar.
- Tiling WMs (i3/sway, no tray protocol without a status bar): the hidden
  window has no affordance back — the only recovery is relaunching Handy
  (the single-instance plugin then shows the window). That is "kill and
  relaunch", not the documented "closing window quits the app".

This becomes more visible as more close affordances are added (e.g. #1914
adds Ctrl+W and a sidebar close button; on i3 + `--no-tray` they hide the
window with no way back), but the mismatch itself predates that PR: the
title-bar close has always hidden instead of quitting under `--no-tray`.

## Suggested resolution (maintainer's call)

Either:

1. Honor the doc: in the shared close path, quit (`app.exit(0)`) when
   `no_tray` is set (mirrors the tray Quit menu), or
2. Change the doc: `--no-tray` keeps the app running and closing merely
   hides; state the recovery path per platform.

Option 1 matches the current AGENTS.md wording; option 2 matches current
behavior. Filing this separately so the semantics decision stays with the
maintainer rather than riding along a UI-fix PR.

